<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!-- THE COMPANY JOURNAL. THIS IS AN ANSWER TO A COMPLAINT THE OWNER MADE WEEKS BEFORE IT COULD
       BE BUILT.

       Owner, 2026-10-03, verbatim, and it is quoted in CompRouteEvidence.cs too: "the company is
       suppose to supply u with a journal to do tasks in but they only gave me noraml books named
       wrong things that dont do anything". Under the existing-content-only direction the only
       available answer was a Core TextBook with our component patched onto it, which is literally
       a normal book named a wrong thing. Owner, 2026-10-06: "and the journal", "journal(s)".

       IT REVIVES A defName RATHER THAN INVENTING ONE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. RR_RouteRecording
       shipped in 0.2.0 and was retired at 0.9.0-dev, but CompRouteEvidence never stopped accepting
       it: IsSupportedCarrier has always taken this exact defName from this exact package, as a
       migration path for saves made before the retirement. So the evidence pipeline already works
       with it, and a save from 0.2.0 finds its recordings still legible. The test used to be
       called IsLegacyCarrier; it is IsCompanyCarrier now, because "legacy" stopped being true the
       moment this def shipped again and a reader trusting the old name would have deleted it as
       dead code.

       WHY IT IS A BOOK AND NOT AN ITEM WITH A BOOK-SHAPED NAME. RimroomsEvidenceCreationComponent
       requires `item is Book` before it will bind a recording, CompanyCarrierDef requires the same,
       and a journal that cannot be read is the complaint again in a different costume. Inheriting
       BookBase brings Core's quality, hauling, deterioration, book tab and stack limit of one, so
       a journal behaves like every other book in the player's colony and every mod that handles
       books handles this.

       CompProperties_Book IS DECLARED HERE AND CANNOT BE INHERITED. BookBase carries CompQuality
       and ITab_Book but NOT CompBook: Core declares that per book, on TextBook, Schematic, Novel
       and Tome separately. CompanyCarrierDef requires a CompBook, so omitting it would make this
       def silently fail to qualify and every caller would fall back to Core's textbook forever -
       working software, wrong book, no error anywhere.

       MATCHING PAPER JOURNAL VIEWS. The closed view is the ground graphic and icon; CompBook uses the
       matching open and vertical facings while a pawn reads. These are original Rimrooms assets,
       with their masters outside the package; no Core or provider texture is copied. This changes
       the journal's presentation, not its Def identity, evidence custody or native book behaviour. -->

  <ThingDef ParentName="BookBase">
    <defName>RR_RouteRecording</defName>
    <label>company field journal</label>
    <description>A bound company field journal with a numbered cover and pages for route notes, sketches and observations. The branch issues journals blank, a crew writes one in the field, and it comes home as the record of where they went and what was there.\n\nAnalysis at a bench turns written findings into a record the company will pay for. An unwritten journal carries no field findings.</description>
    <thingClass>RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Investigation.RimroomsRecordBook</thingClass>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Item/Rimrooms/Journal/RR_CompanyJournal_Closed</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>0.8</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <uiIconPath>Things/Item/Rimrooms/Journal/RR_CompanyJournal_Closed</uiIconPath>
    <!-- **THIS DEF NAME WAS DECLARED TWICE AND THE DUPLICATE WAS SHIPPED.** `RR_FieldEquipment.xml`
         already carried an `RR_RouteRecording` - the found-evidence item from 0.2.0, kept loadable
         for old saves - and this file added a second ThingDef with the same defName, which RimWorld
         resolves by one silently winning. Found by a duplicate scan written after the fact; the
         scan is `tools/check-def-duplicates.py` now, so it cannot happen twice.
         The two are ONE object in this design: the company issues it blank, a crew writes it in the
         field, and it comes home as the finding. So the old def is gone and this one inherits its
         evidence category, which is what the records archive and every evidence filter read. -->
    <thingCategories>
      <li>RR_Evidence</li>
    </thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>180</MarketValue>
    </statBases>
    <comps>
      <li Class="CompProperties_Book">
        <!-- Core's makers, and the label a player sees is still ours. CompBook generates a title
             and a description from these; RimroomsRecordBook exists precisely so that
             CompRouteEvidence.TransformLabel gets a chance to replace what is shown with the
             company's own stamp. Supplying no maker at all would be asking Core to work with a
             null it has never been handed. -->
        <nameMaker>Namer_TextBook</nameMaker>
        <descriptionMaker>Description_SkillBook</descriptionMaker>
        <ageYearsRange>0~1</ageYearsRange>
        <openGraphic>
          <texPath>Things/Item/Rimrooms/Journal/RR_CompanyJournal_Open</texPath>
          <graphicClass>Graphic_Multi</graphicClass>
          <drawSize>0.7</drawSize>
        </openGraphic>
        <verticalGraphic>
          <texPath>Things/Item/Rimrooms/Journal/RR_CompanyJournal_Vertical</texPath>
          <graphicClass>Graphic_Multi</graphicClass>
          <addTopAltitudeBias>true</addTopAltitudeBias>
        </verticalGraphic>
        <doers>
          <!-- Reading somebody's field notes teaches a little. No quest doer: a company journal
               that occasionally hands out an unrelated quest would be the company behaving like a
               wandering trader, and register row 148's disposition is that Rimrooms objectives
               must not depend on the quest system at all. -->
          <li Class="BookOutcomeProperties_GainSkillExp" />
        </doers>
      </li>
      <!-- NO analysisWorkRequired HERE, AND IT WAS REMOVED FROM THE PATCH IN THE SAME CHANGE.
           The figure lives once, as the default on CompProperties_RouteEvidence in C#. Writing
           3000 here would have been a second copy of a tuned number, which is the defect this
           project keeps meeting; the patch on Core's TextBook carried the only other copy and now
           relies on the same default. Both books analyse at the same cost because there is one
           place that says what that cost is. -->
      <li Class="RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Investigation.CompProperties_RouteEvidence" />
    </comps>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
